<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#san-pedro-cactus">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">San Pedro cactus</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Echinopsis pachanoi</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The San Pedro cactus, Echinopsis pachanoi, is a fast-growing columnar cactus native to the Andes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> and Ecuador. It contains mescaline and has been used in Andean healing and religious ceremonies for at least 3,000 years; it appears in the art of Chavín de Huántar, where figures are shown holding it. Its use continues in traditional healing in northern Peru.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/san-pedro-cactus.jpg" alt="San Pedro cactus" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Forest &amp;amp; Kim Starr · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Starr_070320-5799_Echinopsis_pachanoi.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
